\par }{\rtlch \af1\afs15 \ltrch \f1\fs15\insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 A parent who brings his child to the milestones in his life seeks a straight path. He does not want to grope or search for what could be right. Instead
, he wants to take his child by the hand and lead him down a well-paved road.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 This is the advantage of following the guidance of our Jewish tradition. There is ample room for creativity and personal expression, but there are clear guidelines and directives
. A parent takes his child on the path which our people have tread for thousands of years.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 The first steps the child takes down this path are very significant. In that vein, the age of three marks a turning point. Until this point, the child was carried by
 his parents; from this age onward, the parent and child proceed together, hand in hand. Until this age, the child was a baby to whom everything was given. From this time onward, he is considered as a developing youngster who responds and takes an active 
role in his process of education.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Judaism marks this turning point with two customs upsherinish, the child's first haircut, and areinfirinish, the child's entry to cheder and the beginning of his formal education.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch 
\insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 "What exactly should be done?" and "What is the significance of all these customs?" are questions which almost all parents naturally ask as they approach these celebrations.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch 
\insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 To provide answers, we have prepared this publication.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Before concluding it is necessary to acknowledge two publications: Yal
kut HaTisporet by Rabbi Yosef Yitzchak Serebryanski and Kuntres Upsherinish by Rabbi Yosef Yitzchak Simon. These texts provided much of the source material used in this publication. Also, we would like to thank Rabbi Levi Goldstein for his advice with reg
ard to the customs associated with the areinfirinish.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
Our Sages relate that at the splitting of the Red Sea, the culmination of the redemption from Egypt, it was the children who "recognized G-d first." May our efforts in educating our children hasten the c
oming of the day when they will "recognize Him first" at the coming of the ultimate Redemption to be led by Mashiach.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \ab\af1\afs27 \ltrch 
\b\f1\fs27\cf2\insrsid14899619 Inaugurating Education
\par 
\par }\trowd \irow0\irowband0\lastrow \ltrrow\ts11\trqc\trgaph75\trleft-134\trbrdrt\brdroutset\brdrw15 \trbrdrl\brdroutset\brdrw15 \trbrdrb\brdroutset\brdrw15 \trbrdrr\brdroutset\brdrw15 
\trftsWidth2\trwWidth4000\trautofit1\trspdl22\trspdt22\trspdb22\trspdr22\trspdfl3\trspdft3\trspdfb3\trspdfr3\trpaddl75\trpaddt75\trpaddb75\trpaddr75\trpaddfl3\trpaddft3\trpaddfb3\trpaddfr3 \clvertalc\clbrdrt\brdroutset\brdrw15 \clbrdrl\brdroutset\brdrw15 
\clbrdrb\brdroutset\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdroutset\brdrw15 \clcbpat16\cltxlrtb\clftsWidth1\clcbpatraw16 \cellx6992\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
The Rebbe Rashab would say: "Just as we are commanded to put on tefillin every day, we are commanded to spend at least a half an hour each day thinking about our children's education."\cell }\pard \ltrpar
\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \fs20\insrsid14899619 \trowd \irow0\irowband0\lastrow \ltrrow\ts11\trqc\trgaph75\trleft-134\trbrdrt\brdroutset\brdrw15 \trbrdrl\brdroutset\brdrw15 \trbrdrb
\brdroutset\brdrw15 \trbrdrr\brdroutset\brdrw15 \trftsWidth2\trwWidth4000\trautofit1\trspdl22\trspdt22\trspdb22\trspdr22\trspdfl3\trspdft3\trspdfb3\trspdfr3\trpaddl75\trpaddt75\trpaddb75\trpaddr75\trpaddfl3\trpaddft3\trpaddfb3\trpaddfr3 \clvertalc\clbrdrt
\brdroutset\brdrw15 \clbrdrl\brdroutset\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdroutset\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdroutset\brdrw15 \clcbpat16\cltxlrtb\clftsWidth1\clcbpatraw16 \cellx6992\row }\pard\plain \ltrpar
\s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 When a child is born, every parent is overcome with amazement. The conception and birth of new life is an outright miracle. Since this miracle recurs day after day, sometimes we may fa
il to appreciate how awesome it is. But the wonder of holding one's own newborn overwhelms every mother or father, making them appreciate that something that can only be described as G-dly has taken place.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t1}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n1" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 1}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t1}]}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
Together with these feelings of amazement comes a sense of responsibility. Every parent realizes that he or she holds the future of this budding life in their hands. The quality of the child's life depends on his father and mother.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch 
\insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
A parent understands that, and wants the best for his or her child. He or she will make sacrifices - often even painful ones - to provide their children, not only with their basic needs, but with whatever comforts and amenities that are within the
ir reach.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
Parents appreciate, moreover, that the most important gifts they can give their children are internal ones. The material benefits with which they provide them are subject to the vicissitudes of time and the shifting winds of fortunes. The values 
and principles with which they endow them, by contrast, are timeless, serving as a resource and support for the child throughout his life.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s3\qc \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \ab\af0\afs27\alang1025 \ltrch \b\fs27\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkstart caption1}A Lifeline To The Spiritual Source}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend caption1}
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
Judaism refers to the inculcation of these values and principles with the term Chi
nuch. We find that term also used in other contexts. For example, the dedication of the sacrificial altar of the Sanctuary was referred to as Chanukas Hamizbeach. And when a person builds a home, he inaugurates its use with a celebration referred to as Ch
anukas Habayis.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
In those contexts, the term Chinuch refers to connecting an entity to its spiritual source. For example, the Shechinah, G-d's presence, manifested itself within the Sanctuary. The physical structure became subsumed to its spiritual purpose. 
The special sacrifices offered at Chanukas Hamizbeach, made this possible, drawing G-d's presence into this physical world and making the Sanctuary the place for His indwelling.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t2}}
{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n2" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 2}}}{\rtlch 
\af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t2}]}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Similarly, every Jewish home is more than bricks and stones; it is "a sanctuary in microcosm,"}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t3}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch 
\af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n3" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 3}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 
{\*\bkmkend t3}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  a place where each one of us brings out the depth, meaning, and purpose w
ithin his or her existence. In no small part, it is the celebration of Chanukas Habayis which empowers this to take place.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t4}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n4" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 4}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t4}]}{
\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s3\qc \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \ab\af0\afs27\alang1025 \ltrch \b\fs27\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkstart caption2}Teaching A Child To Be Whom He Really Is}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend caption2}
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
Similarly, in a personal sense, Chinuch, the education a child receives, connects him to his spiritual core. Every one of us possesses a soul which is "an actual part of G-d,"}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t5}}
{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n5" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 5}}}{\rtlch 
\af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t5}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  a spar
k of His infinity. Chinuch is the process that enables us to realize and identity with this potential, making it the driving force in our lives.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 To cite a parallel: Emunah, the Hebrew term for "faith," relates to the Hebrew word Imun, meaning "practice" or "training."}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t6}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst 
{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n6" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 6}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t6}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
 The implication is that it is not our understanding and knowledge that spawns our faith. Instead, since the core of our own being is a G-dly spark, we all know 
G-d inherently and therefore we have faith. Emunah involves practice and training, laboring to develop sensitivity to this inner potential and working to create a setting within our conscious minds that will allow this inner knowledge to be easily express
ed.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
Similarly, in a more general sense, Chinuch is the process in which a child is trained to manifest the spiritual potentials with which he has been endowed. Just as Chanukas Hamizbeach made the Sanctuary something far greater than its physical structure;
 so, too, Chinuch enables a person to reach beyond his individual self and manifest the principles and values which Judaism has treasured throughout the ages.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s3\qc \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \ab\af0\afs27\alang1025 \ltrch \b\fs27\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkstart caption3}Starting On Time}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend caption3}
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
A father and mother once asked the Rebbe: "What should we do for our son's education?"}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 "How old is your son?" the Rebbe responded.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 "Six."}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 "Why are you coming to me so late?" replied the Rebbe.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 A child's education begins from the moment he or she enters the world.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t7}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n7" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 7}}}{\*\bkmkend t7}\ltrpar {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t7}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
 For at every moment, the child is learning. His future is being shaped by his past and present. The way he reacts to situations is a product of the way he or she has been trained.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Whether or not we are conscious of it, this training process is t
aking place. It is inescapable. Whatever a child sees or hears affects him. Thinking about a child's chinuch means taking responsibility for this process of development instead of letting it happen randomly, without control.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch 
\insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 The earlier we begin asserting 
direction over this process, the more effective and comprehensive influence we can exert. To cite a parallel, any slight blemish in a seed creates a major imperfection in the tree which grows from it. And conversely, the care and attention lavished over a
 sapling bears fruit as it develops into a flourishing plant.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 For this reason, from the earliest ages, efforts must be devoted to a child's education. Even when he or she does not understand, his character is being shaped. That's why the music played for a 
child, the pictures hung on his walls,}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t8}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 
 HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n8" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 8}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t8}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  and the way he is spoken to, is so important. Although the child does not understand at the time, these and other influences have a gr
eat effect later in life. This rockbed of positive Jewish impressions serves as the foundation for the growth and development of his or her conscious awareness.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s3\qc \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \ab\af0\afs27\alang1025 \ltrch \b\fs27\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkstart caption4}The First Buddings Of Understanding}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend caption4}
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
Although a child's character is being molded from his earli
est age, an entirely new phase begins when the child's intellect begins to blossom. The effects of the influences to which the parents have exposed the child over the years without his conscious input are then given a chance to flourish. He begins tapping
 and channeling the reservoir of positive energy that has been building up over the course of time.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 In this process, the age of three reflects a significant plateau.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t9}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 
 HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n9" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 9}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t9}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  At this 
age, by and large, a child can think in sentences. He or she can comprehend a story. He is aware of his own identity and that of the people around him. As he or she reaches this stage of conceptual development, his or her Chinuch is lifted to a higher run
g. From this time onward, not only is the child being educated, he or she takes a participatory role in his or her process of education.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 For a boy, two events are celebrated to mark the initiation of this new phase: upsherinish and areinfirinish. The upsher
inish marks the child's first haircut. Until this age, his hair is allowed to grow untouched. At this time, his hair is cut, but his peyos, the hair growing at the corners of his head, are left. Similarly, from this time onward, the child is trained to we
ar a yarmulka }{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t10}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n10" }
}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 10}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t10}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  and tzitzis}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t11}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n11" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 11}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t11}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  in a consistent manner.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [
{\*\bkmkstart t12}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n12" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 12
}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t12}]}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
What is the point of these practices? It's like putting on a uniform, using an external code of dress to proclaim your identity. In this way, a child appreciates and makes a statement that he is part of some
thing that is larger than himself. In a very tangible and obvious way, he expresses his commitment to his Jewish heritage.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 The areinfirinish marks a boy's entry into cheder.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t13}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 
 HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n13" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 13}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t13}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  He begins study, learning the letters of the alef-beis.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t14}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 
 HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n14" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 14}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t14}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  This is the first step of his formal intellectual training. He is taught to develop his mind in an environment of holiness on the foundations of faith.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
Both of these events are carried out with a traditional ceremony. The departure from the norm enables the child to appreciate that he is entering a new phase of life. The unique practices make him realize that he is 
embarking on a new and different stage of development and help him take these changes seriously. To state it on the most practical level: After a child has experienced an upsherinish, nothing more than a gentle reminder of that celebration is necessary to
 coax him to wear his yarmulka and tzitzis. And the excitement of the areinfirenish carries over into a child's initial days of study, allowing him to beginning his schooling experience with positive energy.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Similarly, both of these events are experienced a
midst joyful celebration. For happiness opens gateways in the spiritual realms, arousing Divine influences that contribute to the success of the child's education. This enables him to take his place in perpetuating our people's glorious tradition, adding 
one more link in the golden chain of our Jewish heritage, and preparing him to proceed to our people's ultimate celebration, the coming of Mashiach. May this take place in the immediate future.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s3\qc \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \ab\af0\afs27\alang1025 \ltrch \b\fs27\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkstart caption5}What Is An Upsherinish?}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend caption5}
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
It is a long-standing custom for parents to let their sons' hair grow and cut it for the first time at the age of three. Exactly when this custom started is unclear. The students of the Kabbalistic sage, the AriZal (Rabbi Isaac Luria) relate}{\rtlch 
\af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t15}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n15" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 
\ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 15}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t15}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
 that he took his son to the grave of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai, the author of the Zohar, in Meron for this ceremony.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t16}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n16" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 16}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t16}]
}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  They do not speak of the
 Ari's act as an innovation he initiated, but rather as his adherence to an ancient and revered custom. Significantly, this practice is observed in both Sephardic and Ashkenazic communities.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 The ceremony is intended to train a child to observe the mitzvah of peyos,}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t17}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n17" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 17}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t17}]
}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  but as mentioned above, it is considered as the initial phase of a child's conscious Jewish education, carrying far greater significance than the observance of this particular prohibition.}{\rtlch 
\af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Our Rabbis draw a connection between this custom and several other mitzvos. For example, a connection is drawn}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t18}}
{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n18" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 18}}}{\rtlch 
\af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t18}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  to the mitzvah of orlah, the pro
hibition against benefiting from the fruits that grow in the first three years of a tree's life. In the fourth year, by contrast, the happy farmer takes his harvest to Jerusalem, to partake of it in an environment of holiness.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch 
\insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Similarly, the Torah compares man to a tree.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t19}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 
 HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n19" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 19}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t19}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  In the first three years of a child's life, there are no edible fruits - no tangible returns for a parent's endeavors.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t20}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {
\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n20" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 20}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t20}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
 During the fourth year, there are harvests of holiness; the first fruits of the child's education are seen. He begins learning verses from the Torah. This process is inaugurated by reaping - by cutting off his locks of hair.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch 
\insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Others}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t21}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 
 HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n21" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 21}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t21}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  compare it to the mitzvah of reishis hagaz, the gifts of the first shearings of one's herd of sheep to the priests.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t22}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {
\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n22" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 22}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t22}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
 The sages of the Kabbalah, the Jewish mystic tradition, associate sheep with the arousal of Divine mercy and the outpouring of kindness that is untempered by judgment.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Others}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t23}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.si
chosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n23" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 23}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t23}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  note that when the Torah}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t24}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 
 HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n24" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 24}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t24}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  mentions the term Vehisgalach ("And he shall remove his hair"), the letter gimmel is ov
ersized. Now gimmel is numerically equivalent to three, alluding to the fact that there is a removal of hair which is a holy act performed when a child reaches three.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 There are many different customs with regard to the date of the child's first haircut.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t25}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch 
\af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n25" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 25}}}{\*\bkmkend t25}\ltrpar {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t25}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  In Rabbinic literature, it is mentioned that some would permit having the child's first hair cut at the age of thirteen weeks.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t26}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n26" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 26}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t26}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  And other sources, mention delaying the cutting of the hair until the age of five.}{
\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t27}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n27" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch 
\af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 27}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t27}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  In Eretz Yisrael, in most communities the custom 
is to give the child a haircut at three. If, however, the child's birthday is in the summer, many follow the practice of holding the upsherinish on the holiday of Lag BaOmer at the grave site of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai, even if the child's third birthday 
is somewhat before or somewhat after that holiday.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 In the Lubavitch community, the Rebbe instituted the custom of holding the upsherinish on the day of the child's third birthday itself, not before}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [
{\*\bkmkstart t28}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n28" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 28
}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t28}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  or not afterwards.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t29}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch 
\af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n29" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 29}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t29}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  When asked if it was better to wait until an "auspicious day," he commented}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t30}}
{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n30" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 30}}}{\rtlch 
\af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t30}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
 that "Since it is Jewish custom to hold an upsherinish when the child reaches the age of three, this is 'a good an auspicious hour.' Who knows whether it will be possible to choose a time of equivalent Divine favor?"}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t31}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n31" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 31}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t31}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
 If for certain reasons, the celebration accompanying the upsherinish could not be held on the appropriate day, the upsherinish should be held on the child's birthday and the celebration on a day when it is convenient.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t32}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n32" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 32}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t32}]}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Generally, the custom is to hold the upsherinish during the day, after the morning prayers. There are, however, those who hold the celebration at night.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t33}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n33" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 33}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t33}]}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Traditionally, it was customary for an upsherinish to be carried out in a holy place. As mentioned above, the AriZal carried out his son's upsheri
nish in Meron, at the grave site of the holy Sage, Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai. Year after year, particularly on the anniversary of that Sage's passing on Lag BaOmer, but even throughout the year, thousands emulate that practice. Similarly, the Radbaz, a reno
wn Rabbinic authority of the 16th century, speaks}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t34}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 
 HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n34" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 34}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t34}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  of carrying out an upsherinish at the grave of the prophet Samuel. And others would customarily carry out this custom at the grave of Shimon HaTzaddik in Jerusalem.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [
{\*\bkmkstart t35}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n35" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 35
}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t35}]}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 If a person does not live near such a holy place, there are many who hold - or at least begin - the upsherinish in a synagogue o
r house of study. There is no sense of the haircut being considered as inappropriate in such places, for the practice is considered as a celebration associated with a mitzvah.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t36}}
{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/02.htm" \\l "n36" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 36}}}{\rtlch 
\af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t36}]}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
It is customary to have righteous men and sages participate in the upsherinish. Chassidim would frequently take their children to the Rebbe to have him initiate the haircutting. If that was not possible, they would write to the Rebbe who would respond
 with a letter of blessing.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 It is appropriate to invite many guests for this celebration.35 Similarly, frequently, it is enhanced by songs and music.35}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Needless to say, since the upsherinish is a landmark in the child's education, the child should be invol
ved in the preparations for the ceremony. It is valuable to take him to see the upsherinish of several of his friends and relatives to familiarize him with the customs and arouse his eagerness for the time when all eyes will be focused on him, as he begin
s his active participation in his Jewish education.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\qc \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch 
