\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \ai\af0\afs20\alang1025 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 \hich\af77\dbch\af0\loch\f77 53.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li2160\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls54\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 ({\*\bkmkstart n53}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/04.htm" \\l "t53" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs20\ul\cf6\insrsid14899619 Back to text}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend n53}) Igros Kodesh, Vol. XVIII, p. 191ff.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \ab\af1\afs27 \ltrch \b\f1\fs27\cf2\insrsid14899619 The Prohibition Against Shaving The Edges of One's Head
\par 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Leviticus 19:27 states: "Do not round off [the hair] at the edges of your heads." Our Rabbis interpreted the term "edges" to refer to the 
hair between the ears and the hairless portion of the face. Maimonides writes: }{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t54}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 
 HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/05.htm" \\l "n54" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 54}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t54}]}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch 
\insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Our Sages did not determine the amount of hair which must be left at the corners of our temples. We have heard, however, from our elders that one must leave at least forty hairs.}{\rtlch 
\ai\af0\afs15 \ltrch \i\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t55}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs15 \ltrch \i\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/05.htm" \\l "n55" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch 
\ai\af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 55}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs15 \ltrch \i\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t55}]}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 One may remove the [hairs from] the edges [of our heads] with scissors. The prohibition applies only to total removal with a razor.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 In one of his responsa, Maimonides clarifies that the forbidden area is about the size of a thumb. The Shulchan Aruch (Yoreh De'ah 181:1) explains that we are speaking about the area where the skull is joine
d to the jawbone. The Kitzur Shulchan Aruch 170:1 states that the area which is forbidden to be shaven extends until below the ear, the place from which the jaw protrudes.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Although the person who violates the prohibition is the one who actually shaves the 
hair off, if the person having his head shaved moves his head to assist the person doing the shaving, he also is considered as having transgressed. Even if a person does nothing at all, it is forbidden for him to allow the edges of his head to be shaven.}
{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t56}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/05.htm" \\l "n56" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch 
\af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 56}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t56}]}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Maimonides}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t57}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 
 HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/05.htm" \\l "n57" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 57}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t57}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  considers this prohibition as one of the safeguards against idolatry. He exp
lains that it was customary for pagan priests to shave the corners of their heads. Hence, the Torah commanded the Jewish people to shun this practice.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t58}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {
\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/05.htm" \\l "n58" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 58}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t58}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  The Chasam Sofer}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t59}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/05.htm" \\l "n59" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 59}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t59}]
}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  explains this concept further: Since pagans could not have their idols grow hair, it was customary for them to shave their own hair, so that they would resemble the images th
ey worshipped. Others offer different rationales for the practice.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 The rationale, however, is not significant. The prohibition is one of the 613 mitzvos of the Torah,}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t60}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {
\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/05.htm" \\l "n60" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 60}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t60}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  incumbent on all Jewish males, at all times and in all places.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 As mentioned above, the upsherinish is instituted to train the child to observe this prohibition. But the prohibition applies not only in childhood, but throughout our lives. And unfortunately, it 
is violated - sometimes, unknowingly - all too often. For when a barber shaves th\ltrpar e hair at the side of the ears, a transgression, equal in severity to partaking of pork or shrimp, is committed.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 In many communities, because the Torah singled out this portio
n of the hair for distinction, it is customary to allow the hair to grow long, thus creating the "earlocks" (peyos) that have become part of the stereotype image of the observant Jew. Significantly, this custom is practiced by Jews from such far-removed o
rigins as Yemen, Morocco, and Eastern Europe.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 The AriZal, however, would not grow his peyos below his ears. Instead, he would trim them with a scissors.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t61}}
{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/05.htm" \\l "n61" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 61}}}{\rtlch 
\af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t61}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  The Lubavitch custom is to follow this example.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t62}}
{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/05.htm" \\l "n62" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 62}}}{\rtlch 
\af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t62}]}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\qc \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  INCLUDEPICTURE "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/icons/note.gif" \\* MERGEFORMATINET }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 {\*\shppict
