\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/06.htm" \\l "t70" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs20\ul\cf6\insrsid14899619 Back to text}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend n70}) Likkutei Sichos, Vol. II, p. 476.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \ai\af0\afs20\alang1025 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 \hich\af77\dbch\af0\loch\f77 71.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li2160\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls72\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 ({\*\bkmkstart n71}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/06.htm" \\l "t71" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs20\ul\cf6\insrsid14899619 Back to text}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend n71}) So as not to ruin the book.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \ai\af0\afs20\alang1025 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 \hich\af77\dbch\af0\loch\f77 72.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li2160\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls73\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 ({\*\bkmkstart n72}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/06.htm" \\l "t72" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs20\ul\cf6\insrsid14899619 Back to text}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend n72}) C
hagigah 13a (citing the Song of Songs 4:11) uses honey as a metaphor for the mystical dimension of Torah study. Tasting the honey empowers the child to proceed until he masters this dimension of study as well. On another level, the honey implies that the 
words of the Torah the child studies should be sweet and satisfying.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \ai\af0\afs20\alang1025 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 \hich\af77\dbch\af0\loch\f77 73.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li2160\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls74\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 ({\*\bkmkstart n73}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/06.htm" \\l "t73" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs20\ul\cf6\insrsid14899619 Back to text}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend n73}) See Sefer HaSichos 5700, p. 68. The child's study of Chumash is begun in this manner, fulfil
ling our Sages' directive (Midrash Tanchuma, Parshas Tzav, sec. 14, et al): "May the pure ones [children untainted by sin] come and occupy themselves with pure subjects [the book of Vayikra which speaks of the sacrificial offerings and the laws of ritual 
purity].
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \ai\af0\afs20\alang1025 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 \hich\af77\dbch\af0\loch\f77 74.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li2160\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls75\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 ({\*\bkmkstart n74}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/06.htm" \\l "t74" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs20\ul\cf6\insrsid14899619 Back to text}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend n74}) Some also have the custom of adding verse 17 from Chapter 3.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \ai\af0\afs20\alang1025 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 \hich\af77\dbch\af0\loch\f77 75.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li2160\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls76\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 ({\*\bkmkstart n75}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/06.htm" \\l "t75" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs20\ul\cf6\insrsid14899619 Back to text}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend n75}) Deuteronomy 33:4;
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \ai\af0\afs20\alang1025 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 \hich\af77\dbch\af0\loch\f77 76.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li2160\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls77\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 ({\*\bkmkstart n76}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/06.htm" \\l "t76" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs20\ul\cf6\insrsid14899619 Back to text}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend n76}) Grain is also one of the metaphors for the Torah. More particularly, Chitah, "wheat," is numerically equivalent to 22, the number of lett
ers in the Hebrew alphabet. The significance of honey has been described above
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \ai\af0\afs20\alang1025 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 \hich\af77\dbch\af0\loch\f77 77.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li2160\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls78\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 ({\*\bkmkstart n77}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/06.htm" \\l "t77" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs20\ul\cf6\insrsid14899619 Back to text}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend n77}) Oil is also a metaphor for P'nimiyus HaTorah, the inner, mystic dimensions of Torah study.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \ai\af0\afs20\alang1025 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 \hich\af77\dbch\af0\loch\f77 78.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li2160\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls79\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 ({\*\bkmkstart n78}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/06.htm" \\l "t78" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs20\ul\cf6\insrsid14899619 Back to text}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend n78}) Isaiah 50:4-5. The verses should be inscribed in the cake with a toothpick, not written with icing.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \ai\af0\afs20\alang1025 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 \hich\af77\dbch\af0\loch\f77 79.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li2160\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls80\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 ({\*\bkmkstart n79}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/06.htm" \\l "t79" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs20\ul\cf6\insrsid14899619 Back to text}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend n79}) Ezekiel 3:3.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \ai\af0\afs20\alang1025 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 \hich\af77\dbch\af0\loch\f77 80.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li2160\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls81\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 ({\*\bkmkstart n80}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/06.htm" \\l "t80" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs20\ul\cf6\insrsid14899619 Back to text}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend n80}) The child's parents should also give generously to tzedakah on that day.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \ai\af0\afs20\alang1025 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 \hich\af77\dbch\af0\loch\f77 81.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li2160\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls82\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 ({\*\bkmkstart n81}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/06.htm" \\l "t81" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs20\ul\cf6\insrsid14899619 Back to text}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend n81}) See Sefer HaSichos 5701, p. 28. In Sichos Shabbas Parshas Pinchas, 5734, the Rebbe explained that the sweets in fact come from the angel Michael, for he is the source of this sweetness. Th
e people throwing the sweets to the child are acting - perhaps unconsciously - as his agents.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch \ai\af0\afs20\alang1025 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 \hich\af77\dbch\af0\loch\f77 82.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li2160\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls83\adjustright\rin0\lin2160\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 ({\*\bkmkstart n82}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/06.htm" \\l "t82" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \cs16\i\f77\fs20\ul\cf6\insrsid14899619 Back to text}}}{\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch 
\i\f77\fs20\cf2\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend n82}) See Sefer HaSichos 5700, p. 69; Sefer HaSichos 5705, p. 104.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 {\rtlch \ai\af0\afs20 \ltrch \i\f77\fs20\cf2\lbr3\insrsid14899619 \line }{\rtlch \ab\af1\afs27 \ltrch \b\f1\fs27\cf2\insrsid14899619 Prac
tices Associated With A Child's Education Connected With An Upsherinish
\par 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 It is common custom within the Torah community to begin training a child in his observance from the earliest ages. Nevertheless, at a very young age, a child lacks the maturity that w
ould enable the educational process to be carried out in a steady, consistent, and firm manner. To put it in practical terms: a child may be given a yarmulka to wear at a young age, but there are times that he will refuse to wear it. Before his upsherinis
h
, a parent may chose to bypass the issue at times. After the upsherinish, that choice should not be made. Instead, the yarmulka should be considered an integral part of his being. Similarly, in all matters of education, the upsherinish should be the age w
here a systematic and determined program of education begins.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14899619 \rtlch \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0\afs20 
\ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 The letter from the Previous Rebbe to which the Rebbe would always refer with regard to an upsherinish}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t83}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 
\ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/07.htm" \\l "n83" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 83}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 
{\*\bkmkend t83}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  alludes
 to such an approach, stating: "From the day of the haircut [when] peyos are left, it is customary to carefully train the child to wear a tallis kattan and to recite the morning blessings, the Grace after Meals, and the Shema before retiring." Implied is 
that some training may have gone on previously, but "careful" training begins from the upsherinish.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 To refer to some of the specific points mentioned in that letter: From page 2 of Siddur Tehillat HaShem, it appears that the intent is not to recite the enti
re morning blessings, but rather Modeh Ani, the blessing over washing the hands, the verse Torah Tzivah, and the first paragraph of the Shema. For Grace, the intent is not the recitation of the entire Grace, but the statement Brich Rachmana. And for the S
hema upon retiring, the first paragraph of the Shema and the verse: Biyadcha afkid ruchi should be recited.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 With regard to tzitzis, a child may be trained to wear tzitzis before his upsherinish. (The Previous Rebbe was trained from the time he began to walk.)}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t84}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/07.htm" \\l "n84" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 84}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t84}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
 From the upsherinish onward, he should be comfortable with wearing the tzitzis and committed to doing so at all times.}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid14899619 
\par }{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 Similarly, with regard to other matters, the upsherinish marks a turning point. For example, with regard to wearing a yarmulka, children should be trained to begin doing so much earlier.}{\rtlch 
\af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t85}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/07.htm" \\l "n85" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 
\ltrch \cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 85}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t85}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619  (The Rebbe Rashab wrote that the 
Previous Rebbe wore a yarmulka from the day of his bris.)78 From the upsherinish onward, however, extra emphasis should be placed on the matter. Also with regard to Negel Vasser, the morning washing, in his Shulchan Aruch HaRav,}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t86}}{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619  HYPERLINK "http://www.sichosinenglish.org/books/upsherinish/07.htm" \\l "n86" }}{\fldrslt {\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch 
\cs16\f77\fs15\ul\cf2\insrsid14899619 86}}}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 {\*\bkmkend t86}]}{\rtlch \af0\afs20 \ltrch \f77\fs20\insrsid14899619 
 the Alter Rebbe writes that a child's hands should be washed from his bris onward. Nevertheless, from the age of three onward, one should be meticulous about the matter.}{\rtlch \af0\afs15 \ltrch \f77\fs15\insrsid14899619 [{\*\bkmkstart t87}}
